<spell-page "grammar" notebook>
	<header level="1">
		Grammar
		<header>How a component's attributes become Fomantic's class string inside its shadow root.</>
	</>

	<!--
		GRAMMAR:  the old Astro page (`grammar.mdx`, deleted in P7), brought up to date from `docs/grammar.md`
		(the design doc:  every kind, value set and rule).  The old page drew the shadow markup by hand;  here
		every example is a LIVE element, and its description names the class string it renders.
	-->
	<markdown>
		Every `ui-*` element renders SEMANTIC shadow markup that carries Fomantic's class grammar:
		`<ui-button size="small" color="primary" appearance="basic">` renders `<button class="ui small basic primary button">`
		inside its shadow root. `ClassBuilder` writes the class string, driven by each component's
		vocabulary file, which lists every attribute, its kind and its values.

		Every example on this page is a live element. Its description says the class string it renders;
		open your browser's inspector to see it on the shadow element.
	</>

	<section label="Why Keep the Grammar">
		<markdown>
			- Shadow markup keeps Fomantic's classes on semantic elements: `<button class="ui small primary button">`.
			- The CSS is then a mechanical port of Fomantic's `.less`, and the override language for the app
			  stylesheet and `::part()` is the vocabulary people already know.
			- Fomantic matches multi-word phrases with substring selectors (`[class*="four wide"]`), so words come
			  out in a fixed order. Attributes are therefore enumerated per kind, never free text.
			- Class words are ALWAYS canonical English. Translated attribute names and values map back to
			  canonical before they reach `ClassBuilder`.
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Shape">
		<markdown>
			```text
			ui  <size>  <color>  <keyOnly...>  <valueAndKey / keyOrValueAndKey...>  <multiple...>  <width...>  <textAlign>  <verticalAlign>  <noun>  <extra>
			```
		</>
		<markdown>
			- `ui` always, unless the vocabulary says `ui: false` (context-only parts such as `column`).
			- Size, then colour: the value only.
			- Key-only words ALPHABETICAL by attribute name, so the output doesn't depend on vocabulary order.
			- Value-and-key words in vocabulary order; then multiple, width, text and vertical alignment.
			- The noun (`button`, `card`, `column`), then any extra classes (a state such as `active`).
		</>

		<section label="All at Once" sticky="stack" collapsible dividing>
			<example "All at Once" description="`size`, two key-only words and the noun:  `ui small basic primary button`.">
				<button size="small" color="primary" appearance="basic">Save</button>
			</example>
			<example "All at Once" description="Attributes in any order render the same words in the same order: both images are `ui tiny left spaced right floated image`. Fomantic needs `left spaced` and `right floated` spelled exactly so.">
				<segment clearing appearance="basic">
					<image floated="right" spacing="left" size="tiny" src="/components/ui/images/wireframe/square-image.png" alt=""/>
				</segment>
				<segment clearing appearance="basic">
					<image size="tiny" spacing="left" floated="right" src="/components/ui/images/wireframe/square-image.png" alt=""/>
				</segment>
			</example>
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Attribute Kinds">
		<markdown>
			Semantics are copied from Semantic UI React's `classNameBuilders`. The class word ("key") defaults
			to the attribute name with `-` replaced by a space (`very-short` => `very short`); a vocabulary can
			set it explicitly.

			| Kind                                          | Attribute                      | Class                     |
			| --------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------ | ------------------------- |
			| `size`                                        | `size="small"`                 | `small`                   |
			|                                               | `size="medium"`                | nothing: the default size |
			| `color`                                       | `color="red"`                  | `red`                     |
			| `valueOnly`                                   | `state="warning"`              | `warning`                 |
			| `icon`                                        | `icon="user"`                  | nothing: a glyph name     |
			| `keyOnly`                                     | `basic`                        | `basic`                   |
			|                                               | `very-short`                   | `very short`              |
			| `valueAndKey`                                 | `floated="left"` (an image's)  | `left floated`            |
			|                                               | `floated` (bare)               | nothing                   |
			| `keyOrValueAndKey`                            | `pointing`                     | `pointing`                |
			|                                               | `pointing="left"`              | `left pointing`           |
			| `multiple`                                    | `only="mobile tablet"`         | `mobile only tablet only` |
			| `width`                                       | `width="4"`                    | `four wide`               |
			|                                               | `columns="equal"`              | `equal width`             |
			| `textAlign`                                   | `text-align="left"`            | `left aligned`            |
			|                                               | `text-align="justified"`       | `justified`               |
			| `verticalAlign`                               | `vertical-align="middle"`      | `middle aligned`          |
			| `boolean`, `enum`, `string`, `number`, `json` | `href`, `value`, `options` ... | nothing: a typed property |
		</>

		<section label="Size" sticky="stack" collapsible dividing>
			<example "Size" description="`size=\"small\"` emits its value;  `size=\"medium\"` is the default and emits nothing.">
				<button size="small">size="small"</button>
				<button size="medium">size="medium"</button>
				<button size="large">size="large"</button>
			</example>
		</>

		<section label="Colour" sticky="stack" collapsible dividing>
			<example "Colour" description="`color=\"red\"` emits its value:  `ui red button`.">
				<button color="red">color="red"</button>
				<button color="primary">color="primary"</button>
			</example>
		</>

		<section label="Value Only" sticky="stack" collapsible dividing>
			<example "Value Only" description="Where a colour would go, a value of its own:  `state=\"warning\"` => `ui warning message`.">
				<message state="warning">state="warning"</message>
			</example>
		</>

		<section label="Key Only" sticky="stack" collapsible dividing>
			<example "Key Only" description="A bare boolean emits its key.  `basic` => `ui basic button`.  `raised` => `ui raised segment`.">
				<button appearance="basic">basic</button>
				<segment appearance="raised">raised</segment>
			</example>
		</>

		<section label="Value and Key" sticky="stack" collapsible dividing>
			<example "Value and Key" description="`floated=\"left\"` => `ui left floated image`;  a bare `floated` emits nothing.">
				<segment clearing appearance="basic">
					<image floated="left" size="tiny" src="/components/ui/images/wireframe/square-image.png" alt=""/>
					<text paragraph>An image with <text code>floated="left"</text>: the text wraps round it.</text>
				</segment>
			</example>
		</>

		<section label="Key or Value and Key" sticky collapsible dividing>
			<example "Key or Value and Key" description="`pointing` => `ui pointing label`.  `pointing=\"left\"` => `ui left pointing label`.">
				<label pointing>pointing</label>
				<label pointing="left">pointing="left"</label>
			</example>
		</>

		<section label="Multiple" sticky="stack" collapsible dividing>
			<example "Multiple" description="`only=\"mobile tablet\"` => `mobile only tablet only row`.  Narrow your window to swap the rows.">
				<grid>
					<row only="computer">
						<column>
							<segment>only="computer": you're on a computer</segment>
						</column>
					</row>
					<row only="mobile tablet">
						<column>
							<segment>only="mobile tablet": you're on a phone or tablet</segment>
						</column>
					</row>
				</grid>
			</example>
		</>

		<section label="Alignment" sticky="stack" collapsible dividing>
			<example "Alignment" description="`text-align=\"center\"` => `center aligned`.  `vertical-align=\"middle\"` => `middle aligned`.">
				<segment align="center">text-align="center"</segment>
				<grid columns="2" vertical-align="middle">
					<column>
						<segment>vertical-align="middle"<space/>on the grid</segment>
					</column>
					<column>
						<segment>sits in the middle</segment>
					</column>
				</grid>
			</example>
		</>

		<section label="Icon" sticky="stack" collapsible dividing>
			<example "Icon" description="`icon=\"user\"` names a glyph:  no class word, an icon in the shadow root.">
				<button icon="user">icon="user"</button>
			</example>
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Widths">
		<markdown>
			The attribute is `width`, NEVER `wide`. It accepts:

			| Form       | Example                 | Columns (of 16) | Class                              |
			| ---------- | ----------------------- | --------------- | ---------------------------------- |
			| number     | `width="4"`             | 4               | `four wide`                        |
			| word       | `width="four"`          | 4               | `four wide`                        |
			| fraction   | `width="1/4"`, `"3/4"`  | 4, 12           | `four wide`, `twelve wide`         |
			| percentage | `width="25%"`, `"100%"` | 4, 16           | `four wide`, `sixteen wide`        |
			| equal      | `columns="equal"`       | --              | `equal width` (only where allowed) |

			- Inexact values (`1/3`, `33%` => 5.33) snap to the nearest column, with a dev-time warning.
			- Out of range (`0`, `17`, `2/1`) emits nothing, with a dev-time warning.
			- A group's count uses its own word: `columns="3"` => `three column` on a grid, `width="3"` =>
			  `three buttons` on `<ui-buttons>`.
		</>

		<section label="Widths" sticky="stack" collapsible dividing>
			<example "Widths" description="`width=\"4\"`, `width=\"1/4\"` and `width=\"25%\"` all emit `four wide`;  the rest of each row is `twelve wide`.">
				<grid celled>
					<row>
						<column width="4">width="4"</column>
						<column width="12">width="12"</column>
					</row>
					<row>
						<column width="1/4">width="1/4"</column>
						<column width="3/4">width="3/4"</column>
					</row>
					<row>
						<column width="25%">width="25%"</column>
						<column width="75%">width="75%"</column>
					</row>
				</grid>
			</example>
		</>

		<section label="Equal Width" sticky="stack" collapsible dividing>
			<example "Equal Width" description="`columns=\"equal\"` => `equal width`:  the columns share each line.">
				<grid columns="equal" celled>
					<column>One</column>
					<column>Two</column>
					<column>Three</column>
				</grid>
			</example>
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Booleans">
		<markdown>
			- absent => false
			- `""`, `"true"`, `"yes"`, or the attribute's own name (`disabled="disabled"`) => true
			- `"false"`, `"no"`, `"0"` => false: Vue sends `open="false"` when it can't find a property
			- any other present value => true (HTML presence semantics)
			- reflection: true => `""`, false => attribute removed; NEVER `"false"`
		</>

		<section label="Booleans" sticky="stack" collapsible dividing>
			<example "Booleans" description="Unlike a native attribute, `basic=\"false\"` and `basic=\"no\"` mean NOT basic.">
				<button appearance="basic">basic</button>
				<button appearance="basic">basic="yes"</button>
				<button>basic="false"</button>
				<button>basic="no"</button>
			</example>
		</>
		<message size="small">Except <text code>disabled</> on a form control, such as a button, an input or a checkbox: the browser disables a form-associated element whenever the attribute is PRESENT, so <text code>disabled="false"</> still disables it. Remove the attribute instead.</>
	</>

	<section label="Medium">
		<markdown>
			`medium` is a real size meaning "default". It is accepted, validated and reflected like any other
			size, but emits NO class, so `size="medium"` and no `size` render identically, and CSS never needs a
			`.medium` rule.
		</>

		<section label="Medium" sticky="stack" collapsible dividing>
			<example "Medium" description="Both render `ui button`.">
				<button size="medium">size="medium"</button>
				<button>no size</button>
			</example>
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Value Sets">
		<markdown>
			Enumerated values are validated against shared sets (`ValueSets`), or a list in the vocabulary:

			- `hues`: `primary secondary red orange yellow olive green teal blue violet purple pink brown grey black`
			  (extensible)
			- `sizes`: `mini tiny small medium large big huge massive`
			- `positions`: `top left`, `top center`, `top right`, `bottom left`, `bottom center`, `bottom right`,
			  `left center`, `right center`; plus ours, `left top`, `left bottom`, `right top`, `right bottom`
			  (beside the target, lined up with its top or bottom edge: word order matters)
			- `attachments`: `top`, `bottom`, `left`, `right`, `top left`, `top right`, `bottom left`,
			  `bottom right`
			- `alignments`: `left center right justified`; `verticalAlignments`: `top middle bottom`
			- `floats`: `left right`; `devices`: `mobile tablet computer`, `large screen`, `widescreen`
			- `widths`: `1` to `16` (plus words, fractions, percentages)
			- `booleans`: `true false yes no`

			Unknown values are dropped with a dev-time "did you mean" warning: `color="gren"` draws a plain
			button and suggests `green`. Each component's API tab lists the values its attributes take.
		</>
	</>
</spell-page>
